# {{LOOP_NAME}} — Trigger Definition

> The launch contract for this loop. It states what the loop is trying to
> achieve, where its state lives, and exactly how it starts — as a
> machine-readable block a runner (or Prime Agent Desk's Loop Runtime) parses.

---

## Verifiable goal

The loop stops when this predicate holds:

> **{{GOAL}}**

Every run checks this predicate after the verifier passes. If it holds, the loop
writes a final summary to the state file and exits cleanly.

---

## State file

```
{{STATE_PATH}}
```

Every run reads this file at startup and writes it before stopping. If the file
is missing or empty, treat it as a first run and initialize the ledger before
taking any action.

---

## Trigger (machine-readable — the runtime reads this)

Exactly one `loop-trigger` block. Two modes. How each is driven depends on your
runner (see "Running it" below). Keep **one** of the two blocks below and delete
the other.

**Run until done** — iterate back-to-back until the goal predicate holds:

```loop-trigger
mode: run-until-done
```

**Interval** — fire every N minutes when a runner invokes it (`/loop` while your
session is open, or cron/`/schedule` for scheduled cold-start runs):

```loop-trigger
mode: interval
interval_minutes: {{INTERVAL_MINUTES}}
```

---

## Running it

This loop does not start on its own — pick a runner:

- **Interval, while your session is open:** `/loop <interval> run one iteration of
  the loops/{{LOOP_NAME}} loop`.
- **Scheduled cold-start (e.g. every morning):** `/schedule`, or an OS cron entry
  calling `claude -p "run one iteration of the loops/{{LOOP_NAME}} loop, honoring
  HUMAN-GATES.md"`. Do NOT put irreversible-gate loops behind an unattended cron.
- **Run until done, unattended:** a shell loop —
  `until ./loops/{{LOOP_NAME}}/verifier.sh; do claude -p "run one iteration of the
  loops/{{LOOP_NAME}} loop"; done` — with your own iteration cap matching
  `max_iterations`.
- **Manually, one pass:** say *"run one iteration of the {{LOOP_NAME}} loop"* — the
  agent reads `{{STATE_PATH}}` and the loop's `SKILL.md`, does a single pass, runs
  `verifier.sh`, updates state, and stops.

Each pass reads `{{STATE_PATH}}`, does one action, runs `verifier.sh`, writes state,
and **stops at any gate in `HUMAN-GATES.md`**. There is no runtime enforcing the
budget — your runner's cap is the backstop.

See `references/host-claude-code.md` for exact runner commands. (This same
`loop-trigger` block is what Prime Agent Desk's Loop Runtime reads, so the loop
also runs there unchanged.)

---

## Trigger notes

- The budget / stop limits in `HUMAN-GATES.md` take precedence over any interval.
  If the loop hits its iteration, token, or time cap, it halts regardless of how
  often the trigger fires.
- Before enabling an automated trigger, confirm `HUMAN-GATES.md` gates G1
  (pre-run sign-off) and G2 (verifier anomaly) are in place.
- `interval_minutes` only applies when `mode: interval`; omit it for
  `run-until-done`.
